1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,560
Everybody has looked in the mirror at some point in their lives and thought they could do with a smaller nose or fewer wrinkles.
每个人在生活中的某个时刻都曾照过镜子，并想过自己如果能有个更小的鼻子或更少的皱纹就好了。

2
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:16,840
Almost 10 million operations were performed in 2014, according to the International Society of Aesthetic Plastic Surgery.
根据国际美容整形外科学会的数据，2014年进行了近1000万例整形手术。

3
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:24,950
Eyelid surgery topped the list of the five most popular procedures, which included breast augmentation and rhinoplasty.
在五种最受欢迎的手术中，眼睑（眼皮）手术位居榜首，其他还包括隆胸和鼻整形术。

4
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:28,340
We hear a lot about cosmetic surgery these days.
如今我们经常听到关于整容手术的消息。

5
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:34,500
But what many people may not realise is that reconstructing someone's face has an ancient past.
但许多人可能没有意识到，面部重塑手术有着悠久的历史。

6
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,510
There are reports of treatments to restore a broken nose in ancient Egyptian documents.
古埃及文献中就有关于修复鼻梁断裂的治疗方法的记载。

7
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:51,380
Considering that safe anaesthetics and antibiotics were only discovered in the 19th and 20th centuries, it was a truly painful and risky business to go under the knife in the past.
考虑到安全的麻醉剂和抗生素直到19世纪和20世纪才被发现，过去接受手术是一件极其痛苦且危险的事情。

8
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:59,080
But as far back as the mid-15th century nose jobs were carried out using skin taken from the upper arm.
但早在15世纪中叶，鼻部整形手术就已经开始使用从上臂取下的皮肤。

9
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:03,530
Plastic surgery went through a revolution during World War One.
第一次世界大战期间，整形手术经历了一场革命。

10
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:10,580
The trenches protected the soldiers' bodies, but many who stuck their heads up were exposed to explosions.
战壕保护了士兵的身体，但许多探出头的人却暴露在爆炸中。

11
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:21,850
New techniques were developed by Harold Gillies who ran a ward at the Cambridge Military Hospital in Britain, and later a whole hospital dedicated to helping disfigured soldiers.
哈罗德·吉尔斯开发了许多新技术，他在英国剑桥军事医院管理一个病房，后来开设了一整家医院，致力于帮助毁容的士兵。

12
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:26,130
Many wounded men at that time couldn't stand looking at their own faces.
当时许多受伤的士兵无法忍受看到自己的脸。

13
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:28,410
There were no mirrors in the hospital.
医院里没有镜子。

14
00:01:28,410 --> 00:01:35,880
In parks near the hospital, some benches were painted blue to signal to patients with facial injuries that they could sit there.
在医院附近的公园里，一些长椅被涂成蓝色，以向面部受伤的患者示意他们可以坐在那里。

15
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:42,240
And it was a way of telling local residents to prepare for the shock of seeing someone with a disfigured face.
这也是告诉当地居民，让看到毁容的人做好心理准备。

16
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:45,840
Plastic surgery has come a long way since then.
从那时起，整形手术已经取得了长足的进步。

17
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:53,250
It reconstructs what does need reconstructing but it also makes people with no particular problems feel more confident.
它不仅重塑了需要修复的部位，也让那些没有特别问题的人感到更加自信。

18
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:54,460
Bigger breasts?
隆胸？

19
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:55,820
Straight nose?
笔直的鼻梁？

20
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:56,720
Facelift?
面部拉皮？

21
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:01,650
In many cases, cosmetic surgery isn't a necessity at all, just a choice.
在许多情况下，美容手术根本不是必需品，而只是一种选择。

22
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:13,450
But when done properly by capable doctors on patients who have a realistic goal, it's believed that plastic surgery can heal psychological wounds almost as much as physical ones.
但如果由有能力的医生为有现实目标的患者进行手术，人们相信整形手术可以同样有效地治愈心理创伤和身体创伤。
